0 < 1 # CLAUDE.md
1 < 1 
2 < 1 For agents **developing the plugin**; end-user docs are in `README.md`. Path-scoped detail
3 < 1 loads from `.claude/rules/factory.md` (engine) and `.claude/rules/fleet.md` (launcher, VM,
4 < 1 record) when a session reads those trees.
5 < 1 
6 < 1 ## Purpose
7 < 1 
8 < 1 ultrapowers authors a plan and then executes it in parallel. The operator stays closely
9 < 1 involved in **planning** (what to build, how it is verified) and barely in
10 < 1 **implementation**: a disposable exe.dev sandbox runs the plan as a board of tasks claimed by
11 < 1 a pool of builder agents, each task proved by the plan's own `Run:` probes and `Check:`
12 < 1 lines, merges their work continuously through the weave, and opens its own PR. No LLM
13 < 1 orchestrator, no orchestrator VM; the PR is the one gate.
14 < 1 
15 < 1 **One engine: the Flock (map #1292 rule 8).** Every launch boots `factory/flock/engine.mjs`:
16 < 1 a leaderless swarm, an elastic builder pool, one weave replica per builder merged
17 < 1 continuously. The factory it replaced is retired; its rollback is a launch from a checkout
18 < 1 made before the retirement.
19 < 1 
20 < 1 ## Commands
21 < 1 
22 < 1 ```bash
23 < 1 python3 -m pytest                                                            # the test gate (pytest.ini scopes it to tests/; bridges fleet/tests/test_*.mjs)
24 < 1 python3 skills/ultrapowers/scripts/validate_skill.py skills/ultrapowers      # validate a skill dir
25 < 1 python3 skills/ultrapowers/scripts/plan_parse.py <plan.md>                   # what the sandbox reads: tasks, edges, waves, checks
26 < 1 python3 skills/ultrapowers/scripts/plan_check.py --base <sha> <plan.md>      # the laptop's check: records, and the plan against its base
27 < 1 python3 skills/ultrapowers/scripts/run_probes.py <plan.md> [<task id>...]    # a plan's Run: probes on the working tree, one line each (never a hand loop)
28 < 1 bun skills/ultrawrite/stories/compile.ts <bundle> --app <dir> --plan-id <id> --date <YYYY-MM-DD> --out <plan.md>   # a bundle → a stories-v1 plan (the app needs bun install first)
29 < 1 bun skills/ultrawrite/stories/jev_checks.ts <bundle> [--ask-file <ask.txt>]    # code checks, then Jev's; --stage understanding|map|decompose reads product.json
30 < 1 bun skills/ultrawrite/stories/product.ts check|render|record <product.json> …   # the product record: its shape, its page in the operator's words, the operator's picks
31 < 1 bun skills/ultrawrite/stories/arrange.ts <bundle> --app <dir> [--piece <p>] [--pick <V> | --reshape --note <text>…]   # Jev arranges each piece's screen (versions A–C into <bundle>/screens/), a pick, or a reshape from notes
32 < 1 bun skills/ultrawrite/stories/preview.ts <bundle> --app <dir> [--port <n>] [--feedback <file>]   # the real screen for the Browser pane, redrawn as specs change; Comment mode notes go to .ultrapowers/feedback.jsonl
33 < 1 bun factory/stack/tinyapp/check.ts --plan <plan.md> --clause S1.1 --copy <app>   # one story step against one copy (exit 0 pass, 1 finding, 2 could not run)
34 < 1 bun factory/stack/tinyapp/screens.ts (--plan <plan.md> | --bundle <dir>) [--copy <app>] [--piece <piece>]   # every piece's screen spec, no browser (exit 0 pass, 1 finding, 2 could not run)
35 < 1 python3 evals/readings/checker_kit.py                                        # score the checker against a good todo app and broken copies (run when factory/stack/tinyapp/ changes)
36 < 1 node fleet/doctor.mjs --json                                                 # which fleet prerequisite is missing
37 < 1 node fleet/launch.mjs <plan.md> --target <owner>/<repo> --base <sha> --engine <sha>   # one run (the Flock); from this checkout, never the plugin cache
38 < 1 ```
39 < 1 
40 < 1 The plan and census scripts take positional arguments only (no `--help`); the story-planning
41 < 1 tools under `skills/ultrawrite/stories/` (Bun) take flags and print their usage.
42 < 1 Nothing runs on push or on a PR: the sandbox opens a ready PR when the engine's run ended green and a draft otherwise, so
43 < 1 the engine's exit code is the merge decision.
44 < 1 
45 < 1 ## Layout
46 < 1 
47 < 1 - `skills/ultrapowers/` — the operator skill: `SKILL.md` (thin client: commit the plan, launch
48 < 1   the fleet); `scripts/` — `plan_parse.py` (the one plan parser; the sandbox runs it),
49 < 1   `plan_check.py` (the laptop's check on it), `validate_skill.py`; `references/`
50 < 1   (`first-run.md` walks each doctor row); `kernel/` — sha-pinned `vendor/manyana.py`, the
51 < 1   merge the Flock's weave (`factory/flock/weave.py`) runs.
52 < 1 - `skills/ultrawrite/` — plan authoring: the claims-v1 grammar (six body slots, contracts
53 < 1   signed, edges derived, `- Run:` proofs), `references/` (`greenfield-stack.md`,
54 < 1   `authoring-gotchas.md`), `scripts/` (provenance and base-fact pins, `authoring_census.py`),
55 < 1   `stories/` (story planning for TinyApps in TypeScript: bundle loader, code and Jev
56 < 1   checks, the compiler to `stories-v1`, the scaffold, the operator notebook, the screen
57 < 1   arranger and the preview, json-render's composer vendored at `stories/vendor/json-render/`
58 < 1   (Apache-2.0, never patched), and the
59 < 1   TinyApp template: React, json-render screens over 1st-Pouf vendored at
60 < 1   `tinyapp-template/client/src/pouf/`, never patched, like `vendor/manyana.py`), `catalog/`
61 < 1   (ready pieces; `todo` first). The sandbox-facing half is
62 < 1   `skills/ultrapowers/scripts/{probe_block,stories_parse,stories_check}.py`.
63 < 1 - `factory/` — the engine the sandbox runs (see `.claude/rules/factory.md`). Its npm deps are
64 < 1   `factory/package.json`'s.
66 > 1 - `fleet/` — laptop tools (launcher, doctor, token, janitor, run reader) and the VM
65 < 2 - `fleet/` — laptop tools (launcher, doctor, token, janitor, board reader) and the VM
66 < 1   bootstrap (see `.claude/rules/fleet.md`). `fleet/CONTRACT.md` is the authority for every
67 < 1   literal, `fleet/RUNBOOK.md` the operator procedure; the contract wins.
68 < 1 - `hooks/` — wired by `hooks/hooks.json`. `session_start.sh` injects the plan-routing rule
69 < 1   (the rule lives there, not here). `keep_working.sh` is the Stop hook ultrawrite and
70 < 1   ultrapowers declare in frontmatter: once either skill is invoked it blocks a turn's first
71 < 1   stop while background work is running, and allows the second. `register.js` is the intent
72 < 1   tray's hooks module, listed under the `modules` key of `hooks/hooks.json`.
73 < 1 - `.claude-plugin/{plugin.json,marketplace.json}` — manifest and marketplace entry; the
74 < 1   version lives in both.
75 < 1 - `docs/superpowers/{specs,plans}/` — design docs, `YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md`. **Untracked**
76 < 1   (`.git/info/exclude`; `docs/README.md` is the tracked stub). A plan reaches the fleet only
77 < 1   as `.ultrapowers/plan.md`, one commit on the run's base, so a plan may never ask a worker to
78 < 1   read a spec path — the sandbox has none.
79 < 1 - `evals/` — recorded readings and results only. `evals/readings/` holds readings tools no
80 < 1   skill invokes; `evals/fixtures/claims/` is the parser's probe fixture;
81 < 1   `evals/frontier/{corpus,results}/` are gitignored and live on the laptop.
82 < 1 - `tests/` — pytest; `tests/test_fleet_suite.py` bridges every `fleet/tests/test_*.mjs`.
83 < 1 
84 < 1 ## Doctrine
85 < 1 
86 < 1 - **No small measures while broken.** When the product is broken, replace the shape
87 < 1   confidently and keep the old one as the rollback; don't hedge with partial fixes ("if it
88 < 1   doesn't work, we can always roll back" — #589).
89 < 1 - **The engine's values, in tie-break order (map #1131):** the mechanical facts (never
90 < 1   traded), then clock speed, then simplicity in lines and roles, then tokens. **A judgment is
91 < 1   a question, never a sentence or a regex** (each lives in the `questions.json` beside its
92 < 1   reader — `factory/` for the engine, `skills/ultrawrite/stories/` for authoring — and every
93 < 1   threshold is a cell of the `policy.json` beside its reader, with its rollback). Hand work has its own
94 < 1   tie-break (§Working with the operator).
95 < 1 - **Don't vendor the vendor.** Before building a mechanism, ask whether exe.dev already
96 < 1   provides it (identity, edge credentials, the VM comment, tags, first-boot setup script,
97 < 1   cold start). #597 is plain `new` + a setup script for this reason.
98 < 1 - **Ask Shelley before any VM-side hack.** On a papercut on a VM or in a lobby verb, put the
99 < 1   symptom, what was tried and the constraint to Shelley (exe.dev's assistant, on
100 < 1   `fleet-counsel`; `--model=claude-opus-5` reads files; read long answers back with
101 < 1   `shelley client read`) before editing a script. A hack is only a bridge she has blessed.
102 < 1 - **Run in parallel; same-file overlap meets in the weave.** Launch plans concurrently whatever
103 < 1   files they share. A live run follows main (policy `flock.main.mode = peer`): a main that moves
104 < 1   mid-run is merged into the run like a builder's work, and a clash is a conflict a builder
105 < 1   resolves. For a main that moves after the engine exits, a PR whose base moved is refused (405, `strict=true` + `enforce_admins`)
106 < 1   and the sandbox catches the run up to the new main (`factory/flock/catchup.mjs`, on main's stored
107 < 1   weave where the run has one — `factory/flock/weave_store.py`, policy `flock.main.mode` — else through the
108 < 1   weave), so overlaps meet in Manyana (decision 11, #715; read live on flock-baseline runs 16–21,
109 < 1   n=6 runs, 2026-11-13: five ready, one draft). A run whose builder must delete a line main wrote
110 < 1   ends a draft for the operator (#1401's read, operator's pick 2026-11-12). A conflict is the
111 < 1   resolver's measurement, not a reason to serialize. Contention, not allocated vCPU, bounds
112 < 1   concurrent runs.
113 < 1 - **One merge, many writers (#1292).** Every builder writes its own weave replica and merges its
114 < 1   peers' in any order; no engine step is the sole merger. Manyana is the only merge in the system,
115 < 1   so never patch `skills/ultrapowers/kernel/vendor/manyana.py` (sha-pinned). Same-file concurrent
116 < 1   writes are the shipped default. Run state still has one writer, the sandbox, and its record is
117 < 1   git; the laptop only reads it.
118 < 1 - **Handoffs are opt-in.** A session starts from the operator's intention; read
119 < 1   `.claude/ultrapowers/handoffs/` only when asked to resume. Sort by mtime, never filename, and
120 < 1   treat each as what was true when written.
121 < 1 - **Author plans concurrently from the issues:** `skills/ultrawrite/references/authoring-a-queue.md`
122 < 1   — one author per bundle, plans may share files and may create the same new file, the
123 < 1   issue's sentence as the Claim, two operator touches per plan, launches serial.
124 < 1 - **Enrich before drafting (2026-09-28).** Every TinyApp plan runs ultrawrite's own
125 < 1   enrichment (`skills/ultrawrite/references/enrich.md`): intent, understanding, the
126 < 1   whole product's map and the build order, into `product.json`, before any story.
127 < 1   superpowers is never called for it. The operator is asked only what the app does,
128 < 1   who uses it and how it looks.
129 < 1 - **No local process writes run state** (`skills/ultrapowers/SKILL.md` §Client step 5, #660):
130 < 1   the sandbox is the one writer of a run and the launcher reaps by hand after a sleep, so
131 < 1   nothing on a timer here merges, reaps or edits. Reading is telemetry and is wanted: watch a
133 > 1   run with `fleet/run-read.mjs --follow`, in the background or on any cadence (operator,
135 > 1   2026-09-29, narrowing the 2026-09-05 no-timer rule to writers). A run writes to no issue board:
136 < 1   the kata hub left on 2026-11-13, and its rollback is a checkout made before that.
134 < 2   2026-09-29, narrowing the 2026-09-05 no-timer rule to writers). A run writes nothing to the
135 < 2   kata hub (`launch.kata.mode` is `off` in `fleet/policy.json`, 2026-11-13; `on` is the rollback).
132 < 2   run with `fleet/board-read.mjs --follow`, in the background or on any cadence (operator,
133 < 2   2026-09-29, narrowing the 2026-09-05 no-timer rule to writers).
134 < 1 - **Every choice is an AskUserQuestion:** 2–3 concrete options with consequences and a
135 < 1   `(Recommended)` tag, always plus `Please explain` — which re-asks in place with the
136 < 1   explanation written in, escalating from plain words to a before-and-after to a rewrite.
137 < 1   Record every sitting-level question and pick in the plan's `authoring` record; a
138 < 1   recommendation taken every time is retired into a written default.
139 < 1 - **The proof is probes-only.** The implementer never does TDD and writes no test of its
140 < 1   own; a run's proof is the plan's `Run:` probes and `Check:` lines alone (the Flock selects
141 < 1   no existing tests), and the target's suite is a reported sensor. A plan that must keep
142 < 1   existing behaviour names the guarding tests in a `Check:` (e.g.
143 < 1   `python3 -m pytest -q tests/test_fleet_suite.py -k launch`). A test file is pruned by hand,
144 < 1   one per pull request that names what else guards its behaviour.
145 < 1 - **Readings carry their size (#994).** Every reading states its `n=…` and `window`; no
146 < 1   default flips under `n = 5 runs` (`20 tasks` per-task). A flip under the floor is an
147 < 1   `experiment` carrying its `rollback`; a fact read once carries its `date`.
148 < 1 - **Verification is mechanical and fast.** A probe computes facts (exit code, argv,
149 < 1   byte-exact string, count, ordering, oracle agreement); "the code says X" is Jev's, read
150 < 1   against the hunk at landing. A probe is one `Run:` line, one command, ending in the tag of
151 < 1   the clause it proves — an untagged prover settles nothing. One case per behaviour. A run
152 < 1   caught up to a moved main re-runs the plan's setup, probes and check on it. Answer a
153 < 1   gate rejection by narrowing the clause, never by adding legs. A size budget is a note the PR
154 < 1   reports, never a clause. Publish runs after the engine, in plain sequence, never inside it
155 < 1   (`factory/publish.mjs`, #1441).
156 < 1 - **The plan is a submission, not a contract (#990).** A worker that outgrows a clause or its
157 < 1   Files set declares an amendment in the open (`driver:amendment` row, the reviewer's lens,
158 < 1   the PR card, the release census); a reviewer judges it on merits and never reverts it for
159 < 1   being outside the plan. The plan-defect park is only for a leg no implementation can pass.
160 < 1 
161 < 1 ## Working with the operator
162 < 1 
163 < 1 - **They adjudicate, they do not author.** A signed Claim is drafted by the author and confirmed in one touch (draft, machine
164 < 1   restatement and summary in one question); their edit is the Claim. Explain ideas plainly,
165 < 1   not in the technical register.
166 < 1 - **They never read code or tests.** The trust chain is plan → probes → gate receipt → smoke.
167 < 1   Quote receipts; never narrate a green.
168 < 1 - **Hand-work priorities: quality, then tokens, then clock.** The simpler design wins when it
169 < 1   costs none of the three.
170 < 1 - **Times in Pacific, 12-hour clock.** The record stays UTC; chat converts.
171 < 1 - **Propose, then wait for "file it."** Tickets and issue comments follow an explicit ask.
172 < 1 - **Releases are 0.3.x patches** bundling several merges behind a confidence run; a minor bump
173 < 1   only on their call.
174 < 1 - **Three subscriber accounts.** Keychain names are the emails with `@` → `-`; a usage reading
175 < 1   names its account; the rate window is measured, never cited as a bound.
176 < 1 - **Never run `caffeinate`** or touch power settings; fleet runs survive laptop sleep.
177 < 1 - **Pull requests.** The sandbox merges its own. A hand PR uses `gh pr merge --auto --squash`;
178 < 1   never delete a branch while auto-merge is pending (GitHub closes the PR).
179 < 1 - **When an engine gate is broken, route around the fleet:** offer to implement the signed
180 < 1   plan inline first, not a relaunch.
182 > 1 - **The durable record is GitHub issues and PRs and the evidence tags** — Claude's memory
181 < 2 - **The durable record is GitHub issues and PRs, the evidence tags and kata** — Claude's memory
182 < 1   is off. A fact worth keeping goes in an issue comment, the RUNBOOK's Traps, or here.
183 < 1 
184 < 1 ## Conventions & gotchas
185 < 1 
186 < 1 - **Releasing.** Only `plugin.json` carries the version (the marketplace entry has none,
187 < 1   so they cannot drift). A release is one hand PR titled
188 < 1   `chore: version 0.x.y — …` bumping `plugin.json`, squash-merged, then a bare
189 < 1   tag on that commit: `git tag v0.x.y <sha> && git push origin v0.x.y`. **No GitHub release**
190 < 1   until the operator calls it production-ready. Notes go to
191 < 1   `docs/superpowers/plans/<date>-release-0-x-y.notes.md` (untracked) with the census line.
192 < 1 - **The installed plugin is the last release on GitHub, not this checkout (2026-10-03).** The
193 < 1   marketplace is `popmechanic/ultrapowers` (source `github`, `autoUpdate` on), so the cache
194 < 1   holds tracked files only; a local `directory` source copied untracked `.claude/worktrees/`
195 < 1   into every cached version (3.6 GB each). Because `plugin.json` pins `version`, a merge to
196 < 1   `main` changes nothing installed: the plugin updates only when a release bumps the version
197 < 1   and the marketplace refreshes (auto-update, or `claude plugin update ultrapowers@ultrapowers`),
198 < 1   and the new copy loads in the next session or after `/reload-plugins`. Never re-add the
199 < 1   marketplace from the local path. The fleet is different: the sandbox clones the engine at
200 < 1   the launch's `--engine` sha, so `factory/` and `fleet/` changes never need a plugin release.
201 < 1 - **No direct Anthropic API calls in repo code.** No `anthropic` SDK, no `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`;
202 < 1   LLM work runs inside Claude Code on the user's subscription. On the fleet it arrives as an
203 < 1   edge-injected bearer (`ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` → `claude-max.int.exe.xyz`,
204 < 1   `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` a placeholder); `claude auth status` must show `oauth_token` — a
205 < 1   run showing `x-api-key` is billing elsewhere. TypeSafe (`api.typesafe.ai`) is reached the
206 < 1   same way by the boot and engine, and from the laptop by ultrawrite's authoring checks
207 < 1   (`skills/ultrawrite/stories/jev_checks.ts` and `skills/ultrawrite/stories/arrange.ts`, key
208 < 1   in `~/.ultrapowers/typesafe.env`) — for judgments over prose, never generation or facts
209 < 1   (Jev choosing and placing a screen's pieces is a judgment, never generation).
210 < 1 - **Never force-rotate the Claude token while a run is live.** A refresh revokes the old
211 < 1   access token at once and every in-flight run dies with `401 OAuth access token has been
212 < 1   revoked`. While `ssh exe.dev ls` lists a `fleet-r*` VM the token is `not rotated` — by a
213 < 1   launch, a hand `refresh --force`, or a `usage` read — and a launch beside live runs needs
214 < 1   `ninety minutes` or more left on the current sign-in, or refuses. A `usage` read on an
215 < 1   expired account rotates with `install: false` and leaves the edge on a revoked bearer.
216 < 1 - **Fleet sims ride pytest.** `tests/test_fleet_suite.py` runs each `fleet/tests/test_*.mjs`
217 < 1   (sentinel `ALL TESTS PASSED`, 300 s per file, no network — `curl`, `git`, `gh`, `ssh`,
218 < 1   `systemd-run`, `systemctl` are PATH-shim stubs, which keep the network out);
219 < 1   `test_sims_are_hermetic.mjs` catches a sim that inherits the parent's environment, reads an
220 < 1   absolute path on the box, or runs a sibling sim.
221 < 1 - **Superpowers is an optional companion (#390).** Plan authoring is ultrawrite's; superpowers
222 < 1   never enters a sandbox and nothing is vendored. Read its skills from the plugin cache.
223 < 1 - **TinyApp is the name** for a greenfield target on Bun + TypeScript + TinyBase whose
224 < 1   MergeableStore syncs over WsSynchronizer to a Durable Object with a SQLite persister. The
225 < 1   term "vibes app" is banned. Rule: `skills/ultrawrite/references/greenfield-stack.md`.
226 < 1 - **No shouted imperatives** in engine prompts.
227 < 1 - **macOS has no `timeout`**; fleet-counsel's system node is v18 (use `npx -y node@22`).
228 < 1 